<header class="sp-head"><a class="sp-portrait" href="/encyclopedia/girolamo-savonarola" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/girolamo-savonarola.jpg" alt="" width="104" height="104"></a><p class="featured-region">Early 1498 · Florence</p><h1 class="sp-title">Savonarola Defies His Excommunication</h1><p class="sp-by"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girolamo-savonarola">Girolamo Savonarola</a></p></header>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>The setting</h2><p>After the Medici were driven from Florence in 1494, the Dominican friar Girolamo Savonarola, prior of San Marco, became the most powerful voice in the city, preaching repentance, holding bonfires of 'vanities' and attacking the corruption of the Church. In May 1497 Pope Alexander VI excommunicated him and threatened Florence with an interdict. Early in 1498 Savonarola defied the ban and preached again.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>Key passage</h2><blockquote class="sp-quote"><p>I tell you that whoso opposes this work opposes Christ. Understand me well, O Rome!</p></blockquote><p class="sp-source">From his sermons of early 1498, as translated in The World's Famous Orations (1906).</p><div class="sp-full"><button type="button" class="sp-share" data-share-text="“I tell you that whoso opposes this work opposes Christ. Understand me well, O Rome!” — Girolamo Savonarola, Savonarola Defies His Excommunication" data-share-img="/images/og/speech-savonarola-defies-the-pope.jpg">Share this quote</button></div></section>
<section class="sp-sec sp-ft" id="full-text"><h2>The full speech</h2><div class="sp-ft-body"><p>I tell you that whoso opposes this work opposes Christ. Understand me well, O Rome! Whoso opposes this work opposes Christ. O Italy! Whoso opposes this work opposes Christ, O Christian people! If you oppose it you are fighting against Christ, and not against the friar. If you say that the priests of the Church are gathered together against me, I reply that this has come to pass that the prophecies might be fulfilled, even as in our Lord's passion many things were done that the Scriptures might be fulfilled. In this, your hour, you make me perforce a prophet; for you know well that long ago, and repeatedly, I foretold the opposition of the priesthood and of the wicked.</p><p>And I tell you that a new time is coming; and great wars are at hand and we must sustain a more severe contest than that in which we are now engaged. O Lord, I do not ask for peace. War! war! war! is my cry. War, I mean, with the devil; for it is enough to be at peace with Christ. As for the excommunication, which is said by some to be null in the sight of God, but externally binding, it is enough for me that I am not bound in the sight of Christ (da Christo), and that He should bless me. Oh, my Lord, I turn to Thee and I say: If ever I should seek absolution from this excommunication send me to hell! I should fear to commit a mortal sin were I to seek absolution.</p><p>As for those who fear lest they, too, should incur excommunication by coming to hear my sermons, I ask: Is it a sin to preach? Is preaching a crime? If the author of the excommunication were to say so he would contradict the Gospel. Oh, but you say, &quot;I mean because of the disobedience.&quot; And I say that if the law were observed which forbids that any one be made a Doctor of Divinity or a Canon without sufficient learning, there would not be so much ignorance among us.</p></div><p class="sp-ft-credit">Text: <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_World%27s_Famous_Orations/Volume_7/After_His_Excommunication" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>, from The World's Famous Orations (1906). The text is in the public domain; the Wikisource transcription is shared under CC BY-SA 4.0.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>What followed</h2><p>Under pressure from the pope and the city's government he stopped preaching on 18 March. A trial by fire proposed by a rival preacher in April came to nothing and turned Florence against him; he was arrested and tortured, and on 23 May 1498 he and two fellow friars were hanged in the city's main square and their bodies burned.</p></section>
